      "page_title": "Recovery Windows & The Decision Rule",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Three Things To Trust"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reflog undoes anything that moved a ref",
            "No reflog exists for working-tree content",
            "`ORIG_HEAD` = one-step undo from reset, merge, rebase, pull",
            "Stash is commits in disguise — fsck finds it after a drop"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Recovery Window"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Thing",
            "Survives"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Unreachable, via reflog",
              "~30 days"
            ],
            [
              "Prune grace period",
              "2 weeks"
            ],
            [
              "After `gc --prune=now`",
              "gone"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Cheap Insurance"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git branch backup-$(date +%s)  # snapshot",
            "git stash -u  # park everything, untracked too"
          ],
          "note": "plain `git gc` keeps reflog commits"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Commit early and often on a local branch",
            "A commit makes every later mistake reversible"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "8. One Question Decides It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Ask: where does the change ==currently live==?",
            "Unstaged → `git restore <path>`",
            "Staged → `git restore --staged <path>`",
            "Committed, local → `git reset`",
            "Committed and **pushed** → `git revert`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "9. Reset Depth Ladder"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Rewinds"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`--soft`",
              "HEAD only"
            ],
            [
              "`--mixed`",
              "HEAD + Index"
            ],
            [
              "`--hard`",
              "HEAD + Index + Work"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Safety Ranking & Undoing The Undo",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset --soft HEAD~3  # squash",
            "git reset HEAD app.js  # unstage",
            "git reset --hard @{u}  # = remote"
          ],
          "note": "`--hard` destroys uncommitted work"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "10. Safety Ranking"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`revert` — append-only, loses nothing",
            "`restore --staged` — Index only",
            "`reset --soft` / `--mixed` — files safe",
            "`restore <path>` — unstaged edits **gone**",
            "`reset --hard`, then `clean -fdx` — worst: also wipes ==untracked and ignored== files (`.env`, build caches); dry-run with `-n` first"
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "11. Undo The Undo"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reflog  # find the SHA first",
            "git reset --hard HEAD@{1}  # undo",
            "git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD",
            "git branch rescue <sha>  # park it",
            "git fsck --unreachable | grep commit",
            "git stash apply <sha>  # dropped stash"
          ],
          "note": "inspect with `git show` before resetting"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Before Any Rewrite",
          "text": "Push a rewritten branch with `git push --force-with-lease`, never bare `--force` — the lease refuses if the remote moved since your last fetch."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The One True Limit",
          "text": "Everything Git tracks is recoverable except working-tree changes that were never added and never committed. That is the only point of no return."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of GIT UNDO: THE FIELD MANUAL. \"I lost my commits with reset --hard\" is almost always false — the commits are still sitting in your reflog. This field manual walks every undo Git gives you: the three trees underneath them all, reset's three depths, revert for anything already pushed, restore, stash, and fsck rescues for work you thought was gone. Save it for the day your stomach drops.",
